{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F8FDVNo2U7RLtXANLaiCGm","type":"tool_result","content":"1	\section{Discussion}
2	\label{sec:discussion}
3	
4	\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
5	The decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% across models, suggesting a difficulty ceiling. RE's separation may partly reflect its closed-set format; the effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71). Prompt design modulates but does not eliminate the effect (v2 gap: $-$25.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}). Cross-scale evidence (32B vs.\ 8B) shows Channel~1 replicates fully while Channel~2 attenuates on smaller models, dropping to near-zero $R^2$ for NER on Llama-3.1-8B (Tables~\ref{tab:cross_model}--\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Count-driven degradation may partly reflect prompt length (per-type recall 0.586 vs.\ single-pass 0.368, consistent with lost-in-middle effects; \citealt{lost-in-middle-2024}).
6	
7	\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
8	SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space. The unconditional pair-level effect is modest ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$, as ${\sim}89\%$ of pairs never produce mutual confusion); among active-confusion pairs, $R^2 \approx 7\%$ amplifies ${\sim}3\times$ at batch level (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}), yielding $R^2$ of 22--53\%. The NER U-shape (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}) reflects a dual-task burden absent in RE: partial schemas require simultaneous span detection and type-membership filtering; at full-schema, filtering becomes vacuous and recall recovers, confirming task-specific mechanistic pathways.
9	
10	\section{Conclusion}
11	\label{sec:conclusion}
12	
13	Schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM extraction operates through two channels (count-driven recall decline, confusability-driven type confusion) with bounded interaction in RE but coupling in NER. Channel~1 replicates across scales; Channel~2 attenuates on smaller models. BCS enables pre-hoc screening.
14	"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90846268-bc36-4324-9009-c8751031cca1","uuid":"7d4cc843-efff-45d5-83b8-9dd00594bb8a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:59:07.901Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/docs/paper/discussion.tex","content":"\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% across models, suggesting a difficulty ceiling. RE's separation may partly reflect its closed-set format; the effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71). Prompt design modulates but does not eliminate the effect (v2 gap: $-$25.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}). Cross-scale evidence (32B vs.\ 8B) shows Channel~1 replicates fully while Channel~2 attenuates on smaller models, dropping to near-zero $R^2$ for NER on Llama-3.1-8B (Tables~\ref{tab:cross_model}--\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Count-driven degradation may partly reflect prompt length (per-type recall 0.586 vs.\ single-pass 0.368, consistent with lost-in-middle effects; \citealt{lost-in-middle-2024}).

\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space. The unconditional pair-level effect is modest ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$, as ${\sim}89\%$ of pairs never produce mutual confusion); among active-confusion pairs, $R^2 \approx 7\%$ amplifies ${\sim}3\times$ at batch level (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}), yielding $R^2$ of 22--53\%. The NER U-shape (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}) reflects a dual-task burden absent in RE: partial schemas require simultaneous span detection and type-membership filtering; at full-schema, filtering becomes vacuous and recall recovers, confirming task-specific mechanistic pathways.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM extraction operates through two channels (count-driven recall decline, confusability-driven type confusion) with bounded interaction in RE but coupling in NER. Channel~1 replicates across scales; Channel~2 attenuates on smaller models. BCS enables pre-hoc screening.
